    {
      "duration_ms": 10500,
      "caption": "Every exit is a full state switch, so hypervisors work to avoid them.",
      "narration": "An exit saves guest state, loads host state and later reverses it. That's expensive, which is why EPT, virtio and posted interrupts exist: to cut exits.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Why exits matter",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "w",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "EPT: no exit per page-table write",
            "virtio: batch I/O, few kicks",
            "Posted interrupts: no exit to inject"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "color": "orange"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "takeaway": "A hypervisor is a loop around VMLAUNCH/VMRESUME that handles each exit reason and then goes back to native-speed guest execution.",
